    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 172007\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1210 PM PST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry with cooler temperatures through Wednesday. \n\n* A second, colder system will bring another round of unsettled \n  weather to the region late Wednesday through the end of the \n  week. \n\n* Unsettled weather will continue into next week as the weather \n  pattern remains active.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Tuesday.\n\nAn upper-level low is currently swinging through the northern Great \nBasin, with a trailing cold front working through southern Nevada. \nWinds ahead of this front have been rather gusty, with areas across \nnorthern San Bernardino County along with portions of southern \nNevada seeing gusts between 40 and 50 mph. These winds will \ngradually diminish through the afternoon, with the exception of the \nwestern Mojave Desert, which will keep those higher gusts through \nsunset. A Wind Advisory is currently in effect for northern San \nBernardino County and portions of southern Nevada through 5 PM this \nevening, though if the winds continue to diminish, this may be \ndropped earlier. Most of the shower activity that occurred overnight \nand earlier today will continue to shift east, with just a few \nisolated showers remaining over portions of Clark and Mohave \nCounties; however, due to the more favorable dynamics associated \nwith the low, there could be a few thunderstorms this afternoon over \nportions of Lincoln County.\n\nA stronger, colder system will begin to arrive late Wednesday, \nleading to more unsettled weather across the region through Friday. \nThis system is expected to tap into deeper moisture, which will \nlikely lead to widespread heavy snow for the Sierra. A Winter Storm \nWarning remains in effect for the Eastern Sierra from 4 AM Wednesday \nthrough 4 AM Friday, although it looks like the heavier snow won't \narrive until later Wednesday morning or early afternoon. Storm-total \nsnow amounts will be around 22 to 32 inches in Aspendell, CA \n(elevation 8,400 feet), with a higher-end amount of 36 inches. \nPrecipitation will spill over the Eastern Sierra, with moderate \nmountain snow expected in the White Mountains of Inyo County. A \nWinter Weather Advisory is in effect for the White Mountains from \nWednesday morning through Friday morning. Lower elevations will see \nincreased chances for light-to-moderate rain showers on Wednesday \nand Thursday, with the heaviest lower-elevation and valley \nprecipitation expected to occur in the Owens Valley. The primary \nconcerns with late Wednesday and Thursday's rain will be ponding of \nwater in low-lying areas, nuisance flooding, and slick, wet \nroadways. The bigger question will be how much snow will fall in the \nSpring Mountains. At this point, it looks like the trough is \ntrending slightly farther north, which will help put a damper on \nsignificant snowfall for the Springs. The latest model runs have \ndropped snow totals down to around 2 to 4 inches for areas around \nthe town of Mt. Charleston, and up to 6 to 8 inches above 8,500 feet.\n\nAnother concern will be gusty winds ahead of the trough on Thursday \nas both the thermal and surface pressure gradients tighten. Winds \nare expected to increase early Thursday morning and continue through \nmuch of the afternoon, with the strongest winds over southeast \nCalifornia, southern Nevada, and northwest Arizona. Winds gusting up \nto 50 mph will be possible for much of these locations. Wind \nadvisories will potentially be needed for Thursday. \n\nConditions will improve Friday afternoon into Saturday as the trough \nexits the region. Weak, transient ridging will set up across the \narea on Saturday, providing a brief reprieve from unsettled weather \nbefore the next system looks to impact the region early next week as \nthe weather pattern remains active.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast\nPackage...Showers have ended across the area. Gusty winds have ended \nand will remain lighter, generally from the south west becoming more \nsoutherly below 8KT this evening before becoming more light and \nvariable during the early hours Wednesday. There will be a few \nlingering clouds around 5-8kft into early afternoon, but mostly few \nclouds above 20kft and decreasing. Wednesday will see light winds in \nthe morning will some gusty breezes from 15-20KT in the afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Showers have ended \nacross much of the area. Generally decreasing winds and clouds \nthrough the evening and into Wednesday. Windier conditions will \nlinger across San Bernardino County where KDAG will remain gusting \nabout 30KT into the early evening.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Berc\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
